@title  PCSE00084 - boot to a kickoff, kick it, return it, and run the first snap
@game   PCSE00084

# WHAT THIS RECIPE IS FOR
# ----------------------
# The GAMEPLAY recipe for this title: it boots, walks the whole front end, starts a match,
# KICKS OFF and reaches `1ST & 10`. Every frame below was found by playing and is what a
# run actually did; the four gates it clears are each non-obvious, so they are written out.
#
#   1. THE INTRO honours a button only after its own 5.0 s timer ([obj+0x784] climbs about
#      0.0328 a frame, so around frame 165) - and it tests a 30-BIT BITSET OF ITS OWN,
#      indices 10 and 1, which is NOT the SceCtrl bit order. Nobody has mapped that bitset,
#      so the recipe SWEEPS every button rather than guessing which one is index 10.
#
#   2. THE TITLE SCREEN is a separate gate and is only up around FRAME 700. An earlier
#      recipe stopped pressing at 292, so every press it made landed before the screen that
#      wanted one existed, and eight hundred frames sat on "PRESS START BUTTON" looking
#      exactly like a stall.
#
#   3. THE FRONT END is a stack of lists - PLAY NOW, then the game-setup screen (Event Type,
#      Quarter, Accel Clock, Stadium, Weather) and "Advance to Game". The first entry of each
#      is the one that leads to a match, so a `cross` every 120 frames walks it. The match is
#      live by about frame 2,300: the field, the stadium and the HUD reading `5:00 KICKOFF`.
#
#   4. THE `NEW - Motion Sensor Kicking` TIP blocks the kick, and `cross` does NOT dismiss
#      it - the dialog's own options are ⊗ Dismiss / △ Disable Tips / ▢ Open Controls Screen
#      and only TRIANGLE was observed to clear it. Found by sweeping, as gate 1 was.
#
#   5. THE KICK IS THE RIGHT STICK, NOT A BUTTON. The tip's glyphs are analog-stick icons:
#      hold the stick back to raise the power meter, then flick it forward. Every face
#      button, both triggers and the d-pad were swept at the kickoff and NONE of them kicks -
#      the screen does not change at all. `ry=255` held for 60 frames then `ry=1` does.
#
# WHAT A RUN OF THIS LOOKS LIKE, so a regression is recognisable:
#   f002300  the field, the stadium, HUD `5:00 KICKOFF`, the selection reticle on a player
#   f002880  the kick is away - the camera tracks it, the kicker's name is on the HUD
#   f003760  `1ST & 10`, `4:56`, at the 24, play clock `:25` - the return is over
#   f004060+ the snap and a run upfield (sections `hike` and `run`)
#
# KNOWN DEFECTS AT THIS FRONTIER: none visible in the desktop browser as of 2026-09-23 (the
# players, their faces, the HUD, the scoreboard logos and the player-to-watch strip all
# render). What used to be here, so a regression is recognisable:
#   * players not drawn (blocked USSE decodes), a white band across the top, black rectangles
#     for the jumbotron and the play-call panel;
#   * SMEARED FACES / black vertical streaks through heads - two repeat misdecodes in the
#     skinning program (VPCK destination SMLSI slot; group-0x14 repeat count);
#   * WHITE BOXES over the scoreboard team names and the bottom bar, a white play-call panel -
#     the HUD's STENCIL masks, which the renderer did not implement.
#   * A PRESS DURING THE PLAY used to fault in the title's own sprintf (a `%s` whose
#     argument was NULL). That was OURS - a `tbh` whose 29 entries were read as 14, so
#     every conversion from the fifteenth on landed in the default. Fixed; the note stays
#     because the shape of it is worth recognising again.
#
# There is deliberately no @sig: nothing here has been run twice on one build.

0: @section boot

# The intro's own bitset is unmapped, so sweep every button rather than guess.
200: @section sweep-buttons
200: start
206: start
212:
220: cross
226: cross
232:
240: circle
246: circle
252:
260: triangle
266: triangle
272:
280: square
286: square
292:

# The title screen is only up around frame 700 - the gate an earlier recipe missed.
700: @section title-screen
700: start
706: start
712:
760: start
766: start
772:
820: start
826: start
832:
880: start
886: start
892:

# PLAY NOW, then the setup screen, then "Advance to Game". The first entry of each list is
# the one that leads to a match, so a `cross` every 120 frames walks the whole stack.
1000: @section front-end
1000: cross
1006: cross
1012:
1120: cross
1126: cross
1132:
1240: cross
1246: cross
1252:
1360: cross
1366: cross
1372:
1480: cross
1486: cross
1492:
1600: cross
1606: cross
1612:
1720: cross
1726: cross
1732:
1840: cross
1846: cross
1852:
1960: cross
1966: cross
1972:
2080: cross
2086: cross
2092:
2200: cross
2206: cross
2212:

# The match is live from about here. STOP PRESSING: the title is on its kicking tip and a
# `cross` at this point is a press the dialog does not want.
2300: @shot in-a-game

# TRIANGLE - not cross - clears the tip. See gate 4 above.
2600: @section clear-tip
2600: triangle
2614:
2700: @shot at-the-kickoff

# THE KICK. Hold the right stick BACK to raise the power meter, then FLICK it forward.
# 60 frames of hold is what the played run used; the flick only has to last long enough to
# be sampled (the guest samples once per display frame).
2800: @section kick-pull-back
2800: ry=255
2860: @section kick-flick
2860: ry=1
2880:
2900: @shot the-kick-is-away

# The return plays itself out; the down marker turns over to 1ST & 10 by about f3700.
3600: @section first-and-ten
3760: @shot first-and-ten

# THE FIRST SNAP. After the return the offence lines up on its own (f3420 already shows the
# formation at the line) and a `Hike the Ball` tip comes up somewhere around f3920-f4000 -
# its first option is `Dismiss` on cross, and cross is also the hike. So cross is pressed
# THREE times, 60 frames apart: the first dismisses the tip wherever it landed, the next one
# snaps the ball, and a spare covers a tip that arrived late. Then the left stick is held
# UP and the ball carrier runs upfield until the play is whistled dead.
3900: @section settle
3930: @section hike
3930: cross
3936:
3990: cross
3996:
4050: cross
4056:
4060: @shot the-snap
4070: @section run
4070: ly=0
4120: @shot running
4180: @shot running-2
4260: ly=128
4300: @shot after-the-play
4400: @section end-of-play
